        "Eleven companies that earn their money from recipes, approvals, contracts and service rather than from selling a commodity by the tonne. This is one of five groups on the Chemicals page; the others cover the fluorine and refrigerant chain, custom synthesis and specialty intermediates, commodity and petrochemical spread businesses, and carbon, colour and rubber additives. Four make fragrance and personal-care ingredients: **Privi** (pine-based aroma chemicals), **Galaxy Surfactants** (the cleansing agents in shampoos and soaps), **SH Kelkar** (India's largest home-grown fragrance and flavour house) and **Oriental Aromatics** (camphor and aroma chemicals). Four sell formulations: **BASF India**, **Rossari**, **Fineotex** (textile chemicals, and now US oilfield chemicals) and **Chembond** (industrial water treatment). Three are really service or contract businesses: **Ellenbarrie** makes industrial gases, **Premier Explosives** makes missile propellants and countermeasures, and **Refex** now hauls fly ash from power stations. This edition was built from both earnings calls for every company (half-yearly analyst meets at BASF India and Chembond), the FY26 annual reports, rating reports from CRISIL, ICRA and Acuité, and sector work from Crisil, ICRA, CareEdge and Fitch. The headline: Q1 FY27 was a war windfall for **BASF India** and **Galaxy**, and a squeeze for the pine-chemical makers, so trailing profits mislead in both directions. The annual reports turned up more than the calls did: an auditor's qualified opinion on internal controls at **Premier**, a promoter pledge rising to 47% at **Refex**, a ₹445.7cr guarantee at **Galaxy**, a promoter-owned company being merged into **Privi**, up to ₹400cr of loans sought for **Fineotex**'s US subsidiary, promoter pay above consolidated profit at **Oriental Aromatics**, and treasury income worth about two-fifths of **Ellenbarrie**'s pre-tax profit. Every chart uses filed results.",
